  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 246 PM EDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nToday-Tonight... Florida will remain situated between two upper \nlevel high pressure systems, one in the Atlantic and one over TX/SW \nUS, as an upper level trough shifts eastward towards the eastern US \nthrough the day. Shortwave energy will continue to traverse the base \nof this trough over the Deep South and across the Florida peninsula. \nAt the surface, high pressure across the Atlantic will remain nearly \nstationary across south central Florida. Locally, offshore (S-SW) \nflow will persist with speeds 5-10 mph. The east coast sea breeze is \nforecast to form this afternoon, turning the winds onshore (E-SE) as \nthe sea breeze pushes inland. Moisture will remain near to slightly \nabove normal, with PW values of 1.9-2.2\". This will support a medium \nto high (30-60 percent) chance of showers and storms through the \nperiod. CAM guidance shows scattered showers and lightning storms \nforming early to mid afternoon near the coast with the formation of \nthe sea breeze. Coverage is then expected to increase and focus \nacross the interior late afternoon and early evening. Highest \ncoverage of storms will be across the interior with the sea breeze \ncollision. As of 130 PM, scattered showers and storms have formed \nalong the sea breeze across the Treasure coast as well as additional \nstorms moving across Lake county for the west coast of Florida.\n\nThe environment supports storms today, with plenty of instability \n(MUCAPE 1700-2300 J/kg), ample buoyancy (lifted index of -4 to -6), \nand adequate downdraft potential (DCAPE 700-900 J/kg). Main storm \nhazards will be frequent lightning, gusty winds of 45-55 mph, and \nheavy rainfall of 1-3\", which may lead to minor localized flooding \nin urban and poor drainage areas. Any lingering activity will \ndiminish shortly after sundown, with activity across the Atlantic \nwaters continuing through the overnight hours. Temperatures will be \nnear to slightly above normal, with afternoon highs in the low to \nmid 90s with peak heat indices of 100-165F. Overnight lows will be \nin the low to mid 70s.   \n\nSaturday-Thursday... Upper level high pressure over the Atlantic and \nthe SW US will merge over the weekend and remain over the southern \nUS/Gulf/Atlantic into early next week. At the surface, high pressure \nover the Atlantic will continue to shift northward towards the \nCarolinas into early next week. A frontal boundary will slide \nsouthward into the Deep South/Northern Florida this weekend before \nstalling, eventually becoming diffuse by early next week. Locally, \nwinds will shift onshore (easterly) this weekend and remain through \nat least mid next week as the surface high pressure moves northward \nof Florida. Moisture across the local area will remain near to above \nnormal, with PW values of 1.8-2.1\". Daily afternoon showers and \nlightning storms are forecast to continue, with scattered storms \nforming each day along the advancing east coast sea breeze, with \nadditional showers and storms forming across the interior. Highest \ncoverage of storms will be across the interior, where the sea breeze \ncollision is forecast to occur each evening, generally around \nOrlando westward. Storm hazards will be frequent lightning, gusty \nwinds, and locally heavy rainfall. Temperatures will remain \nseasonably hot and humid with with peak heat indices of 100-106, \nwith up to 108 possible in spots next week. Overnight lows will \ncontinue to be warm and muggy.",
